    "instructions": "Read-only access to a Grafana Loki instance. Use these tools to search logs with LogQL (query_range is the workhorse), inspect available labels/values, and gauge log volume via index stats.",
    "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
    "serverInfo": {
      "name": "lokimcp",
      "version": "[version]"
    }
  },
  "prompts": [
    {
      "arguments": [
        {
          "description": "Stream label to match `service` against (default: `job`).",
          "name": "label",
          "required": false
        },
        {
          "description": "Value of the stream label that selects the service, e.g. `varlogs` or\n`caddy`. Combined with `label` to form the stream selector.",
          "name": "service",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "description": "`LogQL` duration for how far back to look, e.g. `1h`, `30m` (default: `1h`).",
          "name": "window",
          "required": false
        }
      ],
      "description": "Scan one service's logs for errors over a bounded time window, then summarise what is failing.",
      "name": "error_scan"
    },
    {
      "arguments": [
        {
          "description": "Optional label to drill into. Omit to start from the full label list.",
          "name": "label",
          "required": false
        }
      ],
      "description": "Explore the label namespace to construct a valid LogQL stream selector before querying.",
      "name": "label_explorer"
    }
  ],
  "rendered_prompts": {
    "error_scan/full": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": {
            "text": "Scan the last <window> of logs for the service `<service>` and tell me what is going wrong.\n\nWork in this order:\n1. Call `label_values` for `<label>` and confirm `<service>` is actually a live stream value. If it is not, stop and say so rather than querying a selector that cannot match — an empty Loki result is indistinguishable from a healthy service.\n2. Call `query_range` with `{<label>=\"<service>\"} |~ \"(?i)error|fatal|panic|exception\"` over the last <window>. `query_range` is the workhorse here; the instant `query` tool answers a different question.\n3. If that returns nothing, widen once — drop the line filter and re-run to confirm the stream is producing logs at all. Distinguish 'no errors' from 'no logs'.\n4. Call `index_stats` for the same selector and window to gauge volume, so you can say whether the sample you looked at is representative or truncated.\n\nReport: the distinct error signatures you found, roughly how often each occurs, the earliest and latest occurrence in the window, and whether the result was capped by the query limit. Quote real log lines. Do not speculate about causes that the logs do not support.",
            "type": "text"
          },
          "role": "user"
        }
      ]
    },
    "error_scan/minimal": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": {
            "text": "Scan the last 1h of logs for the service `<service>` and tell me what is going wrong.\n\nWork in this order:\n1. Call `label_values` for `job` and confirm `<service>` is actually a live stream value. If it is not, stop and say so rather than querying a selector that cannot match — an empty Loki result is indistinguishable from a healthy service.\n2. Call `query_range` with `{job=\"<service>\"} |~ \"(?i)error|fatal|panic|exception\"` over the last 1h. `query_range` is the workhorse here; the instant `query` tool answers a different question.\n3. If that returns nothing, widen once — drop the line filter and re-run to confirm the stream is producing logs at all. Distinguish 'no errors' from 'no logs'.\n4. Call `index_stats` for the same selector and window to gauge volume, so you can say whether the sample you looked at is representative or truncated.\n\nReport: the distinct error signatures you found, roughly how often each occurs, the earliest and latest occurrence in the window, and whether the result was capped by the query limit. Quote real log lines. Do not speculate about causes that the logs do not support.",
            "type": "text"
          },
          "role": "user"
        }
      ]
    },
    "label_explorer/full": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": {
            "text": "Drill into the Loki label `<label>` and help me build a stream selector from it.\n\nStart by calling `label_values` for `<label>`.\n\nThen:\n- Pick the labels that actually partition the data (`job`, `app`, `namespace`, `container` are typical) and call `label_values` on each. Ignore high-cardinality labels like `pod` or `instance` for selector-building — they fragment streams without helping you find anything.\n- Call `series` with a candidate selector to confirm it matches real streams before anyone spends a `query_range` on it.\n- Use `index_stats` on the candidate to show how much volume it covers.\n\nFinish by giving me two or three concrete, copy-pasteable `LogQL` selectors with a one-line note on what each one covers.",
            "type": "text"
          },
          "role": "user"
        }
      ]
    },
    "label_explorer/minimal": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": {
            "text": "Map out this Loki instance's label namespace so I can build a stream selector.\n\nStart by calling `labels` to list every label name.\n\nThen:\n- Pick the labels that actually partition the data (`job`, `app`, `namespace`, `container` are typical) and call `label_values` on each. Ignore high-cardinality labels like `pod` or `instance` for selector-building — they fragment streams without helping you find anything.\n- Call `series` with a candidate selector to confirm it matches real streams before anyone spends a `query_range` on it.\n- Use `index_stats` on the candidate to show how much volume it covers.\n\nFinish by giving me two or three concrete, copy-pasteable `LogQL` selectors with a one-line note on what each one covers.",
            "type": "text"
          },
          "role": "user"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
